# Dockle baseline — accepted best-practice findings, with rationale.
# Dockle runs non-gating (exit-code 0); this keeps the Security tab honest by
# suppressing only findings that are intentional, upstream-only, or systematic
# false positives.

# CIS-DI-0001 "Create a user for the container" / "last user should not be root"
# Intentional across every image. The scanned dev targets (php/node/nginx) and
# the database/broker images require root at startup and drop privileges at
# RUNTIME via their entrypoint (su-exec / gosu) or the compose `user:` directive
# — a privilege drop Dockle cannot observe from static image metadata.
CIS-DI-0001

# DKL-DI-0004 "Use apk add with --no-cache"
# False positive on the UPSTREAM redis:*-alpine build layer (the official image
# compiles redis with `apk add --no-cache` and Dockle misattributes it). Our
# Dockerfile adds only `apk upgrade --no-cache`; /var/cache/apk is empty in the
# final image. Not fixable by us — it is the base image's own layer.
DKL-DI-0004

# CIS-DI-0010 "Do not store credential in environment variables/files"
# Systematic false positive here. This project passes all secrets via Docker
# secret FILES (/run/secrets/...), never via ENV — so every ENV key Dockle flags
# is a standard, non-secret base-image build variable (GOSU_VERSION,
# MARIADB_VERSION, NODE_VERSION, PNPM_VERSION, DEBIAN_FRONTEND, LANG, PATH,
# USER_ID, ...). An accept-key allowlist would have to enumerate every base
# image's ENV and grow with each bump. Actual leaked secret *values* are still
# caught by the dedicated secret scanners (Trivy secret, TruffleHog, GitGuardian,
# GitHub secret scanning), which inspect values, not key names.
CIS-DI-0010
